\begin_layout Standard
I'm a mess.
 I think I just discovered that the health of my social network is abysmal.
 And just like someone who woke up to find themselves in terrible physical condition,
 one step away from hospitalization,
 I need to take steps to perform social network repair.
 This will not be easy.
 I even dread it,
 but I have no choice.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
10 August 2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Slept long,
 slept well,
 feel fine.
 But now I'm going through old lost emails,
 and this sinking sensation appears in my stomach.
 I feel OK,
 but there's like a little birdie whispering in my ear:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Listen to your stomach!
 You should feel melancholic!
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and I kind of even want to,
 to give in,
 to let it settle over me.
 But I'm pushing it away,
 knowing that down that path lies trouble,
 and I've had enough of that,
 thank you very much.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While I dozed into the late morning,
 I again kept thinking about how nothing exists,
 except the here–and–now.
 I continue to find this very mind–blowing;
 its just not how one ordinarily conceives of things,
 because one has memories of the past.
 But those memories are just part of here–and–now,
 of course.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a short time,
 I wondered if here–and–now is a purely subjective experience,
 and that more exists 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

objectively
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 But,
 no,
 that is wrong.
 We have no machines that can travel into the past,
 or future,
 that can perceive the past,
 or future.
 We have no theory for such machines,
 either,
 except for wormholes.
 The here–and–now is a product of physics,
 and not of the mind.
 The subjective experience is apparently in it for the ride.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In short,
 I made no forward progress;
 this was just an echo as I slept.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then I thought of the following diagram,
 from earlier in the diary:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename p10-agents/evolution.eps
	width 40page%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and wondered again how accurate it is to imagine that structure ingresses from some abstract platonic realm,
 into the present here–and–now,
 but then generating pure platonic forms,
 such as the cladogram.
 The point here is that the cladogram 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

does not exist
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 except in the mind of scientists.
 Even species do not exist,
 except as abstract concepts,
 again,
 in the minds of scientists.
 Only specific instances of organisms exist in the here–and–now;
 the fact that some happen to be genetically very nearly identical is an accident of autopoesis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is,
 the above diagram depicts an agency that inhabits the platonic realm only.
 None of the above agent 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

exists
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in the here–and–now,
 in any literal sense.
 However,
 the side effects that this agent has on the arrangement of atoms in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

actual,
 physical,
 real
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 here–and–now is uncontrovertible:
 Darwin noticed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Of course,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

who is Charles Darwin?
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Well,
 he too seems to be a named agent that also operated in the platonic realm.
 That is,
 his brain,
 having a subjective reality,
 like my own,
 peered into information from his sensory organs,
 constructed a world–model (which is encoded with neurons,
 but 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

exists
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in the platonic realm),
 accessed that world model with his subjective–self,
 and spotted a pattern in that world–model.
 Then,
 by hard thought and inspiration,
 he arrived at his theory of evolution,
 which,
 again,
 as a theory,
 exists only in the platonic realm.
 Then he,
 as a subjective individual,
 performed motor actions with his fingers,
 to encode this abstract concept onto ink and paper,
 thus creating a certain complex arrangement of atoms,
 in the here–and–now.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is,
 the human brain,
 perhaps a bit like a ribosome,
 operates in two places at once:
 in the subjective,
 platonic realm,
 and in the physical,
 made–of–atoms realm,
 and leverages it's ability to manipulate physical reality to encode (manifest) platonic ideals as arrangements of physical atoms (in this case,
 ink on paper.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This binding is tight.
 Just as I cannot think without the atoms in my brain,
 so the idea written on a piece of paper cannot exist without the physical paper.
 Insofar as it exists at all,
 it exists in the minds of other humans,
 or,
 in the present case,
 as electronic currents in my laptop.
 And wherever the fuck github stores shit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When I said 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the human brain,
 like a ribosome
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 I was thinking of how a ribosome is made of both RNA and of protein;
 it needs both to bridge the disparate systems.
 The analogy here is that the mind has parts made of atoms,
 and parts made of,
 um,
 subjective reality.
 Is this the case for all agents?
 That they are necessarily bridges between atoms and the platonic realm?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Is evolution a counter–example?
 As drawn in the figure above,
 it is an agent that exists only in the platonic realm.
 And yet,
 it manifests,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

ingresses
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 into the atomic physical world as a collection of biological organisms acting out their lives in the here–and–now.
 Can evolution exist,
 without atoms?
 For the case of Darwinian evolution,
 the answer seems to be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

no
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A different way of thinking about evolution is the computer program that implements,
 for example,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

genetic programming
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 A practical example is MOSES from OpenCog.
 It's an algorithm,
 described in a PhD thesis.
 It's actual,
 compilable,
 runnable code,
 with thousands of variants as it was patched,
 bug–fixed and tuned over time.
 Both the thesis,
 and the code,
 are in the platonic noosphere,
 but they do not exist,
 except as certain arrangements of atoms on certain computers that run certain websites.
\end_layout

